      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wapato was founded in 1885 as a railroad stop on the Northern Pacific Railroad, originally called Simcoe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wapato%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the Chinook Jargon word for an edible root, reflecting the region’s indigenous heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wapato%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wapato grew into a key agricultural hub in the Yakima Valley after the Wapato Indian Reservation was established in 1855.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
